  {
   "where": "main/4",
   "type": "Title",
   "value": "🔋 Understand Batteries with the Tank Analogy",
   "key": null,
   "help": "",
   "proto": {
    "tag": "h1",
    "body": "🔋 Understand Batteries with the Tank Analogy"
   }
  },
  {
   "where": "main/5",
   "type": "Expander",
   "container": true,
   "proto": {
    "label": "🧠 What does this tool show?",
    "expanded": false
   }
  },
  {
   "where": "main/5/0",
   "type": "Markdown",
   "value": "This interactive tool uses the **tank analogy** to make battery behavior easier to understand.\n\nImagine the anode and cathode as two connected **tanks** filled with lithium, like water.\n\n- 🔋 When you **charge** the battery, you pump lithium **uphill** from the cathode tank into the anode tank.\n- ⚡ When you **discharge**, lithium flows **downhill** back to the cathode.\n\nThe **height difference** between the tanks represents the **cell voltage**. It's the potential energy stored in the system.\nEach tank has a unique **shape** (described by its **dQ/dV** curve), which tells us how much lithium it can store at each \"height\".\nIf we record the height difference as the tanks fill, we get a voltage curve. This is exactly what the **Open circuit voltage (OCV)** represents.\nThe overall lithium amount (1 Ah) doesn't change, it's just redistributed between the tanks.  \n\n---\n**Key concepts:**\n- 🔀 **Cell voltage** = height difference between anode and cathode  \n- 📊 **SoC** = how much lithium can move between tanks, bounded by **Vmin** and **Vmax**  \n- 📈 **dQ/dV** = material specific tank shape, shows how much lithium can be stored at a certain voltage level",
   "key": null,
   "help": "",
   "proto": {
    "body": "This interactive tool uses the **tank analogy** to make battery behavior easier to understand.\n\nImagine the anode and cathode as two connected **tanks** filled with lithium, like water.\n\n- 🔋 When you **charge** the battery, you pump lithium **uphill** from the cathode tank into the anode tank.\n- ⚡ When you **discharge**, lithium flows **downhill** back to the cathode.\n\nThe **height difference** between the tanks represents the **cell voltage**. It's the potential energy stored in the system.\nEach tank has a unique **shape** (described by its **dQ/dV** curve), which tells us how much lithium it can store at each \"height\".\nIf we record the height difference as the tanks fill, we get a voltage curve. This is exactly what the **Open circuit voltage (OCV)** represents.\nThe overall lithium amount (1 Ah) doesn't change, it's just redistributed between the tanks.  \n\n---\n**Key concepts:**\n- 🔀 **Cell voltage** = height difference between anode and cathode  \n- 📊 **SoC** = how much lithium can move between tanks, bounded by **Vmin** and **Vmax**  \n- 📈 **dQ/dV** = material specific tank shape, shows how much lithium can be stored at a certain voltage level",
    "element_type": "NATIVE"
   }
  },
